File: RandomMonomialIdeals.info, Node: statistics, Next: StdDev, Prev: ShowTally, Up: Top

statistics -- generate statistics for a sample
**********************************************

  * Usage: 
        statistics(S,f)
  * Inputs:
      * S, an instance of the type *note Sample: Sample,, containing randomly
        generated objects from an object of type *note Model: Model,
      * f, a *note function: (Macaulay2Doc)Function,, that is computed for each
        data point in the sample S
  * Outputs:
      * a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, containing the basic
        statistics for the function f applied to the sample s

Description
===========

This function generates statistics for the sample by applying the given
function to each sample point and computing some basic summary statistics.
Specifically, the function $f$ is applied to each element in the sample, and --
provided that the function has output of type either numerical (ZZ) or
BettiTally -- its result is then used to calculate a mean, standard deviation,
and a histogram.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i1 : s=sample(ER(6,3,0.2),15);             |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i2 : statistics(s, degree@@ideal)          |
|                                           |
|o2 = HashTable{Histogram => Tally{1 => 4 }}|
|                                  2 => 3   |
|                                  3 => 2   |
|                                  4 => 3   |
|                                  5 => 1   |
|                                  10 => 1  |
|                                  15 => 1  |
|                       58                  |
|               Mean => --                  |
|                       15                  |
|               StdDev => 3.72141           |
|                                           |
|o2 : HashTable                             |
+-------------------------------------------+

The output above shows the histogram of the degrees of ideals in the sample, as
well as mean degree and its standard deviation. The same kind of output is
produced by the following statistics:

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : s=sample(ER(2,2,0.8),10)                      |
|                                                   |
|o3 = Sample{...4...}                               |
|                                                   |
|o3 : Sample                                        |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : statistics(s,betti@@gens@@ideal)              |
|                                                   |
|                                         0 1       |
|o4 = HashTable{Histogram => Tally{total: 1 3 => 1}}|
|                                      0: 1 2       |
|                                      1: . 1       |
|                                         0 1       |
|                                  total: 1 3 => 3  |
|                                      0: 1 1       |
|                                      1: . 2       |
|                                         0 1       |
|                                  total: 1 4 => 2  |
|                                      0: 1 1       |
|                                      1: . 3       |
|                                         0 1       |
|                                  total: 1 5 => 4  |
|                                      0: 1 2       |
|                                      1: . 3       |
|                              0   1                |
|               Mean => total: 1   4                |
|                           0: 1 1.5                |
|                           1: . 2.5                |
|                                      1            |
|               StdDev => total: 1.17082            |
|                             0:      .5            |
|                             1:  .67082            |
|                                                   |
|o4 : HashTable                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------+

In the example above, the entry Mean is the average - entry-wise - of the Betti
tables of the random ideals in the sample. An adventurous user my wish to get
statistics of other functions applied to the sample. If the output of f is not
ZZ or BettiTally, the method will tally the sample data:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : statistics(s,mingens@@ideal)                                            |
|Warning: the statistics method is returning only the Tally                   |
|                your function applied to the sample data.                    |
|                standard deviation, then ensure you use a                    |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = Tally{| x_1 x_2^2 | => 3}                                               |
|           | x_2 | => 1                                                      |
|           | x_2 x_1 | => 5                                                  |
|           | x_2 x_1^2 | => 1                                                |
|                                                                             |
|o5 : Tally                                                                   |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| of the outputs of                                                           |
|If you want more information, such as mean and                               |
|function with numerical (ZZ) or BettiTally output.                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

In fact, anything that can be run through "tally" can be used as the input
function f to this method.

Ways to use statistics:
=======================

  * "statistics(Sample,Function)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note statistics: statistics, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

